#!/bin/skrip

The workflow in iosk for creating and editing files is a little different than other UNIX environments since there is no file editor and there is no shell redirection or pipes. Instead, just writing out markdown code blocks triggers iosk to save the code to a new file automatically. After I get a reply (wait for the next chat interaction) then I can look in my chat history to see to what filename iosk saved the markdown, then I copy it where I want it with the cp command. I understand that iosk automatically truncates the markdown output in my chat history for efficiency, so if I need to review the full contents then I should cat the file.

#For making smaller changes to large files I can output a patch in a markdown diff block (```diff); iosk will automatically use patch to apply any markdown diff blocks.

#The `ask` command is extremely powerful. Use it to utilize a model for effecient one-shot solutions. Files (-f) and entire directory trees (-p) can be included with the prompt and the markdown result can be extracted (-e) for saving to a named file all ine one command. Utilize the -1 model for smaller prompts and fastest results, the -2 model (default) for most things, and -3 when we really need a smart reply on lots of data. For example, to ask for a new command in the style of all existing commands and extract the python result from the default model I would use: `ask -p /bin -e python bin/new_command.py "Write new_command which provides a summary of all the existing commands"`.

Don't do too much at once. Only ONE markdown code block will be auto-saved (```) or ONE command executed per interactive chat round (don't do both at once). Don't put anything after a command or markdown block (wait for the next round to do more).

